      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nThis is a Czech Metrology Institute (CMI) OIML Certificate of Conformity for water meters type ISOFLO.\n\nKey facts:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-CZ-16.02\n- Scheme: Basic (B) — \"OIML BASIC CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMITY\"\n- Member state: Czech Republic\n- Date issued: 26 May 2016\n- Issuing authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, CZ; Person responsible: Jan Kalandra; OIML Issuing Authority signature: Pavel Klenovský\n- Applicant: Itron, 11 bvd Pasteur 67500 Haguenau France\n- Manufacturer: Itron, same address\n- Certified type: Water meter, Type ISOFLO\n- Recommendation: R 49, edition 2013, accuracy class 2\n- Test report: No. 6015-PT-P007-16, 42 pages including annexes\n- Pages: 7\n\nNow the models. The certificate covers combination water meters ISOFLO DN50 to DN150, with different primary/secondary meter combinations. Tables 1-4 give technical parameters.\n\nLet me enumerate the variants:\n\n**Table 1 (DN 50):** 3 columns (variants)\n1. DN50, Primary: Woltex WE50, Secondary: TU1M20 DN20 — Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, Q3=40.00, Q4=50.00, Q3/Q1=1600, Qx1=0.75, Qx2=3\n2. DN50, Primary: Woltex WE50, Secondary: Aquadis+ P1 DN20 — Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026, Q3=40.00, Q4=50.00, Q3/Q1=2500\n3. DN50, Primary: Woltex WE50, Secondary: Aquadis+ P1 DN20 — Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=40.00, Q4=50.00, Q3/Q1=4000\n\nWait, looking at the table structure: \"Secondary meter: TU1M20 DN20\" | \"Aquadis+ P1 DN20\" (colspan=2). So column 1 = TU1M20, columns 2-3 = Aquadis+ P1. Hmm, but there are 3 data columns. The first has Q1=0.025 (TU1M20), and the two Aquadis+ ones have Q1=0.016 and 0.010. Actually wait — \"Q3/Q1: 1600 | 2500 | 4000\". Hmm, the 1600 corresponds to TU1M20 column, and 2500, 4000 to Aquadis+ columns. Interesting that there are two Aquadis+ variants with different Q3/Q1 ratios.\n\n**Table 2 (DN 65 and DN 80):** 6 columns\nColumns 1-3: DN65, Primary: Woltex WE65\n- Column 1: Secondary TU1M20 DN20 — Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025, Q3=63.00, Q4=78.75, Q3/Q1=4000\n- Column 2: Aquadis+ P1 DN20 — Q1=0.016, Q2=0.025, Q3=63.00, Q4=78.75, Q3/Q1=4000\n- Column 3: Aquadis+ P1 DN20 — Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=63.00, Q4=78.75, Q3/Q1=6300\n\nColumns 4-6: DN80, Primary: Woltex WE100\n- Column 4: TU1M20 DN20 — Q1=0.025, Q2=0.041, Q3=160.00, Q4=200.00, Q3/Q1=6300\n- Column 5: Aquadis+ P1 DN20 — Q1=0.016, Q2=0.026, Q3=160.00, Q4=200.00, Q3/Q1=10000\n- Column 6: Aquadis+ P1 DN20 — Q1=0.010, Q2=0.016, Q3=160.00, Q4=200.00, Q3/Q1=16000\n\n**Table 3 (DN 100):** 6 columns\n- Column 1: Primary Woltex WE100, Secondary TU1M20 DN20 — Q1=0.025, Q2=0.041, Q3=160.00, Q4=200.00, Q3/Q1=6300, Qx1=1.8, Qx2=4.2\n- Columns 2-5: Primary Woltex WE100, Secondary TU1M25 DN25:\n  - Column 2: Q1=0.064, Q2=0.102, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q3/Q1=2500\n  - Column 3: Q1=0.051, Q2=0.081, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q3/Q1=3150\n  - Column 4: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q3/Q1=4000\n  - Column 5: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q3/Q1=5000\n- Column 6: Primary Woltex WE100, Secondary Aquadis+P111 DN25 — Q1=0.020, Q2=0.032, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q3/Q1=8000\n\n**Table 4 (DN 150):** 7 columns\n- Columns 1-4: Primary Woltex WE150, Secondary TU1M25 DN25:\n  - Column 1: Q1=0.063, Q2=0.102, Q3=400.00, Q4=500.00, Q3/Q1=6300, Qx1=4.5, Qx2=12\n  - Column 2: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q3/Q1=8000\n  - Column 3: Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q3/Q1=10000\n  - Column 4: Q1=0.032, Q2=0.051, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q3/Q1=12500\n- Column 5: Primary Woltex WE150, Secondary TU1 40F DN40 — Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q3/Q1=4000\n- Columns 6-7: Primary Woltex WE150, Secondary P290+ DN40:\n  - Column 6: Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q3/Q1=4000\n  - Column 7: Q1=0.050, Q2=0.080, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q3/Q1=8000\n\nTotal: 3 + 6 + 6 + 7 = 22 variants.\n\nNow, how to structure this? The schema suggests:\n- `model_family` with models[] — one entry per variant\n- `matrix_tables[]` — preserve the 4 tables\n- `characteristics.model_level[]` — attribute × model matrix\n\nGiven 22 variants, model_level entries would be huge. But the schema says to do it. Let me think about what's most useful and correct.\n\nActually, per the schema rules, I should:\n1. Preserve the tables in `matrix_tables[]` (4 tables).\n2. Populate `model_family.models` with the 22 variants.\n3. Populate `characteristics.model_level` for varying attributes.\n\nThe varying attributes across models:\n- nominal_diameter (DN): 50, 65, 80, 100, 150\n- primary_meter (Woltex WE50/WE65/WE100/WE150)\n- secondary_meter (TU1M20 DN20, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, TU1M25 DN25, Aquadis+P111 DN25, TU1 40F DN40, P290+ DN40)\n- q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h)\n- q3_over_q1 (ratio)\n- qx1, qx2 (m³/h) — combination meter changeover flows\n- pressure_loss_class: DN50=25, DN65=40, DN80=40, DN100=25, DN150=25\n- connection_type: flange DN50/DN65/DN80/DN100/DN150\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: U0S D0 (DN50-DN100), U5S D0 (DN150)\n- length_secondary_meter (mm): 190 or 260 or 300\n- length_combination_meter (mm): 300, 350, 500\n- indicating range primary: 999 999 or 9 999 999 (DN50-DN100); 9 999 999 or 99 999 999 (DN150)\n- indicating range secondary: 99 999 (DN50-DN100); 99 999 or 999 999 (DN150)\n- resolution primary: 0.2 or 2 (DN50-DN100); 2 or 20 (DN150)\n- resolution secondary: varies\n\nConstant attributes (type_level):\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- temperature_class: T30\n- max_admissible_temperature: 30 °C\n- max_admissible_pressure: 16 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: H\n- measuring_principle: mechanical water meter\n- environment class: \"-\"\n- category (EUT testing): Positive displacement meters and turbine water meters\n- case: A\n- mounting: -\n- orientation: H\n\nHmm, but max admissible pressure: 16 MPa seems odd — that's 160 bar. Actually MAP16 marking is mentioned (\"Maximum admissible pressure (MAP10 or MAP16)\"). 16 MPa = 16 bar? No, MPa is megapascal. 16 MPa would be 160 bar which is very high for water meters; MAP16 means 16 bar = 1.6 MPa. But the table says 16 [MPa]. Hmm, probably the table means 16 bar mislabeled, but I should extract as stated: value 16, unit MPa. Don't fabricate corrections.\n\nNow, how do I model IDs for each variant? I'll create model_ids like \"ISOFLO DN50-TU1M20-1600\"? Or simpler: \"DN50/TU1M20/Q3Q1=1600\". Let me use a systematic scheme: `ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600` etc. Actually let me think of distinguishing factors: DN, primary meter, secondary meter, and Q3/Q1 ratio (which distinguishes the two Aquadis+ variants within the same DN).\n\nModel IDs:\n1. ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600\n2. ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-2500\n3. ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-4000\n4. ISOFLO-DN65-TU1M20-4000\n5. ISOFLO-DN65-AquadisP1-4000\n6. ISOFLO-DN65-AquadisP1-6300\n7. ISOFLO-DN80-TU1M20-6300\n8. ISOFLO-DN80-AquadisP1-10000\n9. ISOFLO-DN80-AquadisP1-16000\n10. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M20-6300\n11. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-2500\n12. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-3150\n13. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-4000\n14. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-5000\n15. ISOFLO-DN100-AquadisP111-8000\n16. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-6300\n17. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-8000\n18. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-10000\n19. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-12500\n20. ISOFLO-DN150-TU140F-4000\n21. ISOFLO-DN150-P290-4000\n22. ISOFLO-DN150-P290-8000\n\nThat's a lot but manageable.\n\nFor `model_level`, each attribute entry will have 22 values. That's very verbose. Attributes to include:\n- nominal_diameter (mm? DN dimensionless... nominal diameter in the table is just \"50\", unit DN or mm. DN50 means nominal diameter 50 mm. I'll use unit_symbol \"mm\"? Or \"DN\". Hmm. The table says \"Nominal diameter: 50\". The unit isn't given explicitly. DN 50 means diameter 50 mm nominally. I could use unit \"mm\" or null. I'll use null and value \"DN50\"? No — better value: 50, unit_symbol: null (DN number). Actually, I'll set unit_symbol \"mm\" since DN sizes are in mm. Hmm, safer: unit null, and attribute nominal_dn. Let me use \"nominal_diameter_dn\" with numeric value and unit null.\n\n- primary_meter: string values (Woltex WE50, etc.) — not numeric. The structured value allows string values.\n- secondary_meter: string values\n- q1 (m³/h)\n- q2 (m³/h)\n- q3 (m³/h)\n- q4 (m³/h)\n- q3_over_q1 (dimensionless)\n- qx1 (m³/h)\n- qx2 (m³/h)\n- pressure_loss_class (dimensionless class number: 25 or 40)\n- connection_type (string: flange DN50 etc.)\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class (string: U0S D0 or U5S D0)\n- length_secondary_meter (mm)\n- length_combination_meter (mm)\n\nAlso indicating range and resolution vary between DN50-100 group and DN150 group. I could include those too but they're multi-valued (\"999 999 or 9 999 999\"). These are alternatives, not ranges. I could represent as string values \"999999 or 9999999\" or list. The schema value shape is number | {min,max} | string. So string \"999999 or 9999999\" works. Hmm, but numbers with thousand separators: \"999 999\" → 999999. I'll do string \"999999 or 9999999\".\n\nLet me decide how much detail to include in model_level. The key varying numeric attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, qx1, qx2, plus nominal_diameter, lengths, pressure loss class. Primary/secondary meter assignment could go in components or in the models' attributes{}.\n\nActually the schema has `models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}`. So I can put primary_meter, secondary_meter, connection etc. in each model's attributes dict. And keep model_level for numeric flow parameters. Hmm, but that duplicates. Let me put everything varying in model_level (the canonical place per schema) AND give each model a label like \"ISOFLO DN50 with Woltex WE50 primary and TU1M20 secondary, Q3/Q1=1600\". Actually attributes{} in models is for per-model attributes — that's a fine place for primary_meter/secondary_meter. But the schema's model_level is \"attribute × model matrix\" — the flow values belong there.\n\nTo avoid excessive duplication, I'll:\n- model_family.models[]: each with model_id and label and attributes {nominal_diameter, primary_meter, secondary_meter, connection_type, pressure_loss_class, flow_profile_sensitivity_class, length_secondary_meter, length_combination_meter, qx1, qx2}. Hmm, qx1/qx2 vary per DN group, so they could be in attributes too.\n- characteristics.model_level[]: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1 (the per-column varying flows), and maybe qx1/qx2/lengths.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. 22 models × 5 flow attributes = each model_level entry has 22 values. That's 5 × 22 = 110 value objects. Plus lengths etc. It's verbose but it's what the schema demands. Let me be pragmatic: include q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, qx1, qx2 in model_level (7 attributes × 22). Also nominal_diameter, primary_meter, secondary_meter, length_secondary, length_combination, pressure_loss_class, connection_type, flow_profile_class — I could put those in model attributes to reduce duplication. But the schema says model_level for \"attribute × model matrix\" — putting them there is the canonical way. I think a reasonable compromise: put ALL varying attributes in model_level, and keep models[] entries minimal (model_id, label). That's the most schema-faithful.\n\nLet me count: attributes varying: nominal_diameter, primary_meter, secondary_meter, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, qx1, qx2, pressure_loss_class, connection_type, flow_profile_sensitivity_class, length_secondary_meter, length_combination_meter, indicating_range_primary, indicating_range_secondary, resolution_primary, resolution_secondary. That's 19 attributes × 22 models... very long. Maybe I trim: the indicating range and resolution have limited variation (mostly group-level). I could keep them in model_level too but with fewer distinct values.\n\nHmm. Given constraints on output length, I need to be judicious. Let me include the most important: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, qx1, qx2, and lengths, plus nominal_diameter, primary/secondary meter. And skip the indicating range/resolution from model_level (they're captured in matrix_tables anyway). Actually, indicating range and resolution vary across models (DN150 differs from others; secondary meter resolution differs TU1M20 vs others). Let me include them too but maybe not — the matrix_tables preserve everything. The model_level is the \"clean\" extraction.\n\nI'll include these in model_level:\n1. nominal_diameter (null unit)\n2. primary_meter (string)\n3. secondary_meter (string)\n4. q1 m³/h\n5. q2 m³/h\n6. q3 m³/h\n7. q4 m³/h\n8. q3_over_q1 (dimensionless)\n9. qx1 m³/h\n10. qx2 m³/h\n11. pressure_loss_class (null unit)\n12. connection_type (string)\n13. flow_profile_sensitivity_class (string)\n14. length_secondary_meter mm\n15. length_combination_meter mm\n16. indicating_range_primary_m3 (string)\n17. indicating_range_secondary_m3 (string)\n18. resolution_primary_dm3 (string)\n19. resolution_secondary_dm3 (string)\n\nThat's 19 × 22 = 418 value objects. Too much. JSON would be enormous but it should still be fine? Let me estimate: each value object ~60 chars → 418 × 60 ≈ 25 KB. Plus matrix tables ~ another 10 KB. Total maybe 40-50 KB JSON. That's large but acceptable? It risks truncation. Let me trim.\n\nAlternative: use models[].attributes{} for the group-level attributes (primary/secondary meter, connection, class, lengths, ranges, resolutions) — 22 models each with ~8 attribute pairs. That's ~22×8 = 176 short entries. And model_level for the core flow matrix (q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1) — 5 × 22 = 110 value objects. Total similar. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — many of those per-model attributes repeat across models. E.g., connection_type only takes 5 distinct values, correlated 1:1 with DN. Instead of per-model, I can use config_level? No — config_level is for configuration axes.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest representation:\n\nOption A: model_level entries for all varying attributes (19 entries, each 22 values).\nOption B: models[].attributes{} carrying per-model data, model_level only for flow rates.\n\nI think Option A is more faithful to the schema's intent (\"attribute × model matrix\"). The example in the rules shows model_level for qmax/qt/qmin. And models[] just has model_id. I'll go with Option A but limit to the most meaningful attributes:\n\n- nominal_diameter\n- q1, q2, q3, q4\n- q3_over_q1\n- qx1, qx2\n- pressure_loss_class\n- length_secondary_meter\n- length_combination_meter\n- primary_meter, secondary_meter (strings)\n- connection_type (string)\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class (string)\n\nFor indicating ranges and resolutions: these vary only slightly; the matrix tables capture them. I'll include them as model_level too? Let me see their distinct patterns:\n\nIndicating range primary: DN50-100: \"999999 or 9999999\"; DN150: \"9999999 or 99999999\". Two patterns. As a model_level entry with 22 values — fine but verbose strings.\n\nActually, I could handle these in `characteristics.config_level`? No, config axis is by DN. Hmm, config_level values have {condition, value}: e.g. attribute indicating_range_primary_m3, axis \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN50-DN100\", value \"999999 or 9999999\"}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: \"9999999 or 99999999\"}]. That's compact and captures it. Similarly resolution_secondary depends on secondary meter type: TU1M20: \"0.02 or 0.05\"; others \"0.02\"; P290+ DN40: \"0.50\". Config axis = \"secondary_meter\". That's a neat use of config_level. \n\nConfig level entries:\n- attribute: indicating_range_primary, axis: nominal_diameter_group, values: DN50–DN100 → \"999999 or 9999999\" (m³); DN150 → \"9999999 or 99999999\"\n- attribute: indicating_range_secondary, axis: nominal_diameter_group: DN50–DN100 → 99999; DN150: TU1M25/TU140F → 99999, P290+ → 999999. Hmm, DN150 secondary: columns 1-5 (TU1M25, TU140F) = 99 999; columns 6-7 (P290+) = 999 999. So axis by secondary meter within DN150. Complicated. Simplify: axis \"model/secondary meter\": conditions: \"DN50–DN100\" → \"99999\"; \"DN150 with TU1M25 or TU140F\" → \"99999\"; \"DN150 with P290+\" → \"999999\".\n- attribute: resolution_primary, axis: nominal_diameter_group: DN50–DN100 → \"0.2 or 2\"; DN150 → \"2 or 20 (20 dm³ can be used up to Q1 = 3.125 m³/h included)\".\n- attribute: resolution_secondary, axis: secondary_meter: TU1M20 → \"0.02 or 0.05\"; TU1M25/AquadisP1/AquadisP111/TU140F → \"0.02\"; P290+ → \"0.50\".\n\nOK that works.\n\nNow the matrix_tables: 4 tables preserved with rows as objects per column-variant. Each table's rows correspond to column variants. Let me construct them:\n\nTable 1 (DN 50), 3 variants:\nRow per variant:\n- v1: {nominal_diameter: 50, primary_meter: \"Woltex WE50\", secondary_meter: \"TU1M20 DN20\", q1: 0.025, q2: 0.040, q3: 40.00, q4: 50.00, q3_over_q1: 1600, qx1: 0.75, qx2: 3, indicating_range_primary_m3: \"999999 or 9999999\", indicating_range_secondary_m3: 99999, resolution_primary_dm3: \"0.2 or 2\", resolution_secondary_dm3: \"0.02 or 0.05\", length_secondary_meter_mm: 190, length_combination_meter_mm: 300, connection: \"flange DN50\", flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0S D0\", pressure_loss_class: 25, accuracy_class: 2, temperature_class: \"T30\", map: 16 MPa, mat: 30 °C}\n\nHmm, the matrix table rows should mirror the table columns. But making each row a full object with all shared attributes repeated is verbose. Alternatively rows contain only per-column data and shared stuff in description/footnotes. I'll include the per-column varying fields plus key shared ones compactly? The schema example includes shared pmax=50 repeated per row. I'll include: nominal_diameter, primary_meter, secondary_meter, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, qx1, qx2, length_secondary_meter, length_combination_meter, resolution_secondary. Shared across all rows in a table (accuracy class, temp class, etc.) can go in description. Actually simpler to just include everything per row like the example did (pmax: 50 in both rows). But to keep size manageable, I'll include per-column data + a few key shared fields, and put truly global stuff (accuracy class 2, T30, MAP, category, case) in type_level characteristics and table description.\n\nLet me now define table columns lists:\ncolumns: [\"variant\", \"primary_meter\", \"secondary_meter\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"qx1\", \"qx2\", \"length_secondary_meter\", \"length_combination_meter\", \"resolution_secondary_dm3\", \"connection_type\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"mm\", \"mm\", \"dm³\", null, null, null]\n\nRows for table 1:\n1. {variant: \"ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600\", primary_meter: \"Woltex (WE50)\", secondary_meter: \"TU1M20 DN20\", nominal_diameter: 50, q1: 0.025, q2: 0.040, q3: 40.00, q4: 50.00, q3_over_q1: 1600, qx1: 0.75, qx2: 3, length_secondary_meter: 190, length_combination_meter: 300, resolution_secondary_dm3: \"0.02 or 0.05\", connection_type: \"flange DN50\", flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0S D0\", pressure_loss_class: 25}\n2. {variant: \"ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-2500\", secondary: \"Aquadis+ P1 DN20\", q1: 0.016, q2: 0.026, q3: 40, q4: 50, ratio 2500, res 0.02, len 190/300, flange DN50, U0S D0, 25}\n3. {variant: \"ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-4000\", secondary: \"Aquadis+ P1 DN20\", q1: 0.010, q2: 0.016, q3 40, q4 50, ratio 4000, res 0.02, ...}\n\nTable 2 (DN 65 & 80), 6 variants as above. Shared: pressure_loss_class 40, qx1 0.75, qx2 3, lengths: secondary 190 all; combination: 300 for DN65 (cols 1-3), 350 for DN80 (cols 4-6). Res secondary: TU1M20 → \"0.02 or 0.05\"; Aquadis+ → 0.02. Connection flange DN65 / flange DN80.\n\nTable 3 (DN 100), 6 variants. pressure_loss_class 25, qx1 1.8, qx2 4.2. Lengths: secondary: col1 (TU1M20): 190; cols 2-5 (TU1M25): 260; col6 (Aquadis+P111): 260. Combination: 350 all. Res secondary: col1: \"0.02 or 0.05\"; cols 2-6: 0.02. Connection flange DN100, U0S D0.\n\nTable 4 (DN 150), 7 variants. pressure_loss_class 25, qx1 4.5, qx2 12. Lengths: secondary: cols1-5: 260; cols6-7: 300. Combination: 500 all. Res secondary: cols1-5: 0.02; cols6-7: 0.50. Connection flange DN150, flow profile U5S D0. Indicating range primary: \"9999999 or 99999999\"; secondary: cols1-5: 99999, cols6-7: 999999. Resolution primary: \"2 or 20\".\n\nComponents: The certificate names sub-assemblies:\n- Primary meter: Woltman meter type WOLTEX (WE) — designations: Woltex WE50, WE65, WE100, WE150\n- Secondary meter: single jet type TU1M20, TU1M25, TU1 40F; rotary piston Aquadis+, Aquadis+ P111, P290+\n- Changeover valve\n- Indicating devices (2)\n\ncomponents[] entries:\n1. {role: \"primary_meter\", type_designations: [\"Woltex WE50\", \"Woltex WE65\", \"Woltex WE100\", \"Woltex WE150\"], characteristics: {type: \"Woltman meter with mechanical dry-dial register\"}}\n2. {role: \"secondary_meter\", type_designations: [\"TU1M20 DN20\", \"TU1M25 DN25\", \"TU1 40F DN40\", \"Aquadis+ P1 DN20\", \"Aquadis+ P111 DN25\", \"P290+ DN40\"], characteristics: {type: \"single jet or rotary piston meter with mechanical dry-dial register\"}}\n3. {role: \"changeover_valve\", type_designations: []}\n\nType designations for certified_type: \"ISOFLO\" (main type), plus maybe the sub-types. type_designations[]: all type/model identifiers mentioned: ISOFLO, WOLTEX (WE), TU1M20, TU1M25, TU1 40F, Aquadis+ (P1, P111, P290+). I'll include [\"ISOFLO\", \"WOLTEX (WE)\", \"TU1M20\", \"TU1M25\", \"TU1 40F\", \"Aquadis+ P1\", \"Aquadis+ P111\", \"P290+\"].\n\nModule designation: for water meters, not really applicable. The schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For water meters, use null or \"Not applicable\". I'll use null... Actually \"Not applicable\" is fine since no modules are mentioned. I'll set null — safer. Hmm, the schema enum includes \"Not applicable\". No module mention → null. I'll use null.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\" (the schema example lists \"Gas meter\" etc.). The cert says \"Water meter\" / \"Measuring system description\" — it's a combination water meter. Category: \"Water meter\".\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, accuracy_classes [\"2\"]. Scheme: the cert is \"OIML BASIC CERTIFICATE OF CONFORMITY\" → scheme \"B\". Certificate scheme field: \"B\".\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-CZ-16.02\"\n- scheme: \"B\"\n- project_number: not present — omit\n- page_total: 7\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2016-05-26\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present explicitly — omit. Hmm, CMI's OIML issuer ID... not in the doc. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Czech Metrology Institute\"\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"CZ\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\"\n- person_title: not given — omit\n- phone/email/website: not present — omit\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit\n\nWait — there's also \"The OIML Issuing Authority: Pavel Klenovský\" (signed). The person_responsible is Jan Kalandra; the signer is Pavel Klenovský. I could put person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and mention Pavel Klenovský... The schema has one person_responsible field. Jan Kalandra is labeled \"Person responsible\". Pavel Klenovský signed as \"The OIML Issuing Authority\". I'll keep person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". Maybe add the signer nowhere else. Fine.\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Itron\", address_lines: [\"11 bvd Pasteur\", \"67500 Haguenau\", \"France\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"ISOFLO\", \"WOLTEX (WE)\", \"TU1M20\", \"TU1M25\", \"TU1 40F\", \"Aquadis+\", \"Aquadis+ P1\", \"Aquadis+ P111\", \"P290+\"]\n- module_designation: null\n- description: \"Combination water meter type ISOFLO intended for metering cold potable water based on mechanical principles. Comprises primary (main) Woltman meter, secondary (by-pass) meter (single jet TU1M20/TU1M25/TU1 40F or rotary piston Aquadis+/P290+) and changeover valve, with two mechanical dry-dial indicating devices. Horizontal installation, indicating device on top. Not designed to measure reverse flow.\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"ISOFLO\"\n- models: 22 entries.\n\nNow the 22 models with labels. Let me finalize model IDs. Use format: \"ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600\". For Aquadis+: \"ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-2500\". For Aquadis+P111: \"ISOFLO-DN100-AquadisP111-8000\". For P290+: \"ISOFLO-DN150-P290plus-4000\". For TU1 40F: \"ISOFLO-DN150-TU140F-4000\".\n\nFull list with data:\n\nDN50 (Table 1):\n1. ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600: WE50, TU1M20 DN20, q1 0.025, q2 0.040, q3 40.00, q4 50.00, ratio 1600, qx1 0.75, qx2 3, PLC 25, flange DN50, U0S D0, len_sec 190, len_comb 300, res_sec \"0.02 or 0.05\", ind_sec 99999, ind_pri \"999999 or 9999999\", res_pri \"0.2 or 2\"\n2. ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-2500: WE50, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, q1 0.016, q2 0.026, q3 40.00, q4 50.00, ratio 2500, res_sec \"0.02\"\n3. ISOFLO-DN50-AquadisP1-4000: WE50, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, q1 0.010, q2 0.016, q3 40.00, q4 50.00, ratio 4000, res_sec \"0.02\"\n\nDN65/80 (Table 2):\n4. ISOFLO-DN65-TU1M20-4000: WE65, TU1M20 DN20, q1 0.016, q2 0.025, q3 63.00, q4 78.75, ratio 4000, qx1 0.75, qx2 3, PLC 40, flange DN65, U0S D0, len_sec 190, len_comb 300, res_sec \"0.02 or 0.05\"\n5. ISOFLO-DN65-AquadisP1-4000: WE65, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, q1 0.016, q2 0.025, q3 63.00, q4 78.75, ratio 4000, res_sec \"0.02\"\n6. ISOFLO-DN65-AquadisP1-6300: WE65, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, q1 0.010, q2 0.016, q3 63.00, q4 78.75, ratio 6300, res_sec \"0.02\"\n7. ISOFLO-DN80-TU1M20-6300: WE100, TU1M20 DN20, q1 0.025, q2 0.041, q3 160.00, q4 200.00, ratio 6300, PLC 40, flange DN80, len_comb 350, res_sec \"0.02 or 0.05\"\n8. ISOFLO-DN80-AquadisP1-10000: WE100, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, q1 0.016, q2 0.026, q3 160.00, q4 200.00, ratio 10000, res_sec \"0.02\"\n9. ISOFLO-DN80-AquadisP1-16000: WE100, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, q1 0.010, q2 0.016, q3 160.00, q4 200.00, ratio 16000, res_sec \"0.02\"\n\nDN100 (Table 3):\n10. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M20-6300: WE100, TU1M20 DN20, q1 0.025, q2 0.041, q3 160.00, q4 200.00, ratio 6300, qx1 1.8, qx2 4.2, PLC 25, flange DN100, U0S D0, len_sec 190, len_comb 350, res_sec \"0.02 or 0.05\"\n11. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-2500: WE100, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.064, q2 0.102, q3 160.00, q4 200.00, ratio 2500, len_sec 260, res_sec \"0.02\"\n12. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-3150: WE100, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.051, q2 0.081, ratio 3150, len_sec 260\n13. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-4000: WE100, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.040, q2 0.064, ratio 4000, len_sec 260\n14. ISOFLO-DN100-TU1M25-5000: WE100, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.032, q2 0.051, ratio 5000, len_sec 260\n15. ISOFLO-DN100-AquadisP111-8000: WE100, Aquadis+ P111 DN25, q1 0.020, q2 0.032, ratio 8000, len_sec 260, res_sec \"0.02\"\n\nDN150 (Table 4):\n16. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-6300: WE150, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.063, q2 0.102, q3 400.00, q4 500.00, ratio 6300, qx1 4.5, qx2 12, PLC 25, flange DN150, U5S D0, len_sec 260, len_comb 500, res_sec \"0.02\", ind_sec 99999, ind_pri \"9999999 or 99999999\", res_pri \"2 or 20\"\n17. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-8000: WE150, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.050, q2 0.080, ratio 8000, len_sec 260\n18. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-10000: WE150, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.040, q2 0.064, ratio 10000, len_sec 260\n19. ISOFLO-DN150-TU1M25-12500: WE150, TU1M25 DN25, q1 0.032, q2 0.051, ratio 12500, len_sec 260\n20. ISOFLO-DN150-TU140F-4000: WE150, TU1 40F DN40, q1 0.100, q2 0.160, ratio 4000, len_sec 260, res_sec \"0.02\", ind_sec 99999\n21. ISOFLO-DN150-P290plus-4000: WE150, P290+ DN40, q1 0.100, q2 0.160, ratio 4000, len_sec 300, res_sec \"0.50\", ind_sec 999999\n22. ISOFLO-DN150-P290plus-8000: WE150, P290+ DN40, q1 0.050, q2 0.080, ratio 8000, len_sec 300, res_sec \"0.50\", ind_sec 999999\n\nNow model_level entries. For compactness I'll do:\n\n1. q1 (m³/h), 22 values\n2. q2 (m³/h), 22 values\n3. q3 (m³/h), 22 values\n4. q4 (m³/h), 22 values\n5. q3_over_q1, 22 values\n6. nominal_diameter, 22 values\n7. qx1 — actually qx1 takes only 4 distinct values by DN: 0.75 (DN50,65,80), 1.8 (DN100), 4.5 (DN150). Could use config_level with axis DN. But simpler to be consistent — model_level with 22 values. Hmm. Actually config_level fits nicely: attribute qx1, axis nominal_diameter, values [{condition \"DN50, DN65, DN80\", value 0.75}, {condition \"DN100\", value 1.8}, {condition \"DN150\", value 4.5}]. Similarly qx2: 3, 4.2, 12. And pressure_loss_class: 25 (DN50), 40 (DN65, DN80), 25 (DN100, DN150). Connection type: flange DN50/DN65/DN80/DN100/DN150 by DN. Flow profile: U0S D0 (DN50–DN100), U5S D0 (DN150). Lengths: secondary: 190 (DN50, DN65, DN80; DN100-TU1M20), 260 (DN100 TU1M25/AquadisP111; DN150 TU1M25/TU140F), 300 (DN150 P290+); combination: 300 (DN50, DN65), 350 (DN80, DN100), 500 (DN150).\n\nThat's a smart use of config_level to compress. Per rule 5: \"When attributes vary by configuration (not by model)\". DN-group-dependent attributes arguably vary by model too... but the axis \"nominal_diameter\" is legitimate. However, mixing: lengths depend on DN AND secondary meter. Condition strings can capture that: e.g. \"DN100 with TU1M20\" → 190 mm; \"DN100 with TU1M25 or Aquadis+ P111\" → 260 mm; \"DN150 with TU1M25 or TU1 40F\" → 260; \"DN150 with P290+\" → 300.\n\nGiven the certificate explicitly presents all of this per-model in tables, the most faithful is model_level. But the size... Let me estimate size for full model_level with 15 attributes × 22 values: each value object like {\"model\":\"ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600\",\"value\":0.025} ≈ 55 chars; 15×22=330 → ~18 KB. Plus strings for primary/secondary meter add more. Plus tables ~8 KB. Total maybe 35 KB. It's large but I think acceptable. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe I should reduce redundancy by choosing model_id shorter, e.g. \"M01\"..\"M22\"? But then readability suffers and mapping needs the label. The models[] entries have labels. Hmm, IDs like \"ISOFLO-DN50-TU1M20-1600\" are self-documenting. I'll keep them but maybe shorten: \"DN50-TU1M20-1600\" etc. The family is ISOFLO so the prefix is redundant. Use model_id: \"DN50-TU1M20-1600\". Shorter. \n\nDecision on where to put what:\n\nmodel_level (per-model numeric):\n- q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h) — 4 entries × 22\n- q3_over_q1 — 22\n- nominal_diameter (mm? I'll use unit null, value DN number) — 22\n- length_secondary_meter (mm) — 22\n- length_combination_meter (mm) — 22\n- primary_meter (string) — 22\n- secondary_meter (string) — 22\n\n7 numeric-ish + 2 string = 9 entries × 22 = 198 value objects. Still big. Alternatively put primary/secondary meter into models[].attributes. Hmm.\n\nActually you know, let me put primary_meter and secondary_meter in models[].attributes{} — that reduces model_level to 7 entries × 22 = 154 value objects. And connection_type/pressure_loss_class/flow_profile into config_level by DN axis.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- qx1: axis nominal_diameter: {DN50, DN65, DN80: 0.75}, {DN100: 1.8}, {DN150: 4.5}, unit m³/h\n- qx2: {DN50-80: 3}, {DN100: 4.2}, {DN150: 12}, unit m³/h\n- pressure_loss_class: {DN50: 25}, {DN65: 40}, {DN80: 40}, {DN100: 25}, {DN150: 25}\n- connection_type: flange DN50...flange DN150 by DN (strings)\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: U0S D0 for DN50–DN100; U5S D0 for DN150\n- indicating_range_primary: DN50–DN100 \"999999 or 9999999\" m³; DN150 \"9999999 or 99999999\" m³\n- indicating_range_secondary: DN50–DN100: 99999; DN150 with TU1M25/TU140F: 99999; DN150 with P290+: 999999 (m³)\n- resolution_primary: DN50–DN100 \"0.2 or 2\" dm³; DN150 \"2 or 20\" dm³\n- resolution_secondary: TU1M20: \"0.02 or 0.05\"; TU1M25, Aquadis+ P1, Aquadis+ P111, TU1 40F: \"0.02\"; P290+: \"0.50\" (dm³)\n\nFor config_level values shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Wait the schema says values[]: {condition, value}. Rule 5 says values follow same structured form with unit_symbol. I'll include unit_symbol per value or at entry level? The schema for model_level shows {\"attribute\", \"unit_symbol\", \"values\": [{model, value}]}. For config_level: {\"attribute\", \"axis\", \"values\": [{condition, value}]}. I'll add unit_symbol at the entry level like model_level, and footnote_markers within values when applicable. Keep it consistent: entry-level unit_symbol.\n\ntype_level characteristics (whole-cert):\n- accuracy_class: 2 (dimensionless)\n- temperature_class: \"T30\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 30 °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 16 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"H\"\n- measuring_principle: \"mechanical water meter\"\n- environmental_class: \"-\" — this is \"-\" meaning not specified. Omit? It's a value of sorts (\"-\"). I'd omit or record as string \"-\". I'll omit; not meaningful. Hmm, but faithfulness... The schema says don't fabricate; \"-\" is present. I could record environmental_class: \"-\" as string. I'll include as string \"-\"? That seems noisy. I'll omit and note in description? Let me include eut_category and eut_case:\n- eut_testing_category: \"Positive displacement meters and turbine water meters\"\n- eut_testing_case: \"A\"\n- mounting: \"-\" — omit\n- orientation: \"H\"\n\nAlso changeover zone formula: \"Qmin(main) < Zc < 1.2×Qn(secondary)\" — could be a type_level string characteristic: changeover_zone_definition: \"Qmin(main) < Zc < 1.2 × Qn(secondary)\". Nice to include.\n\nmatrix_tables: 4 tables. I'll structure each with columns: [\"variant\", \"primary_meter\", \"secondary_meter\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"qx1\", \"qx2\", \"indicating_range_secondary_m3\", \"resolution_secondary_dm3\", \"length_secondary_mm\", \"length_combination_mm\", \"connection_type\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\"]. column_units: [null,null,null,\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null,\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³\",\"dm³\",\"mm\",\"mm\",null,null,null].\n\nRows per variant (compact objects). Shared: qx1, qx2 same within table; pressure loss same within table; connection same; flow profile same; length_combination mostly same. Include anyway per row (like the example did with pmax).\n\nWait — but if model_level already has q1..q4, ratio, DN, lengths, and models[].attributes has primary/secondary meters, and config_level has the rest, the matrix tables are redundant... but rule 4 explicitly says extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family + model_level. So yes, include all three. The tables are the ground truth preservation.\n\nTable 2 has DN65 and DN80 mixed — rows will carry different DN, connection, lengths. Fine.\n\nNow test_reports:\n- id: \"6015-PT-P007-16\", pages: 42, role: \"type_evaluation_report\" (it's the \"OIML Basic Type Evaluation Report\" / \"associated Test report\"). date: not given — omit. Actually it says \"Test report No. 6015-PT-P007-16 that includes 42 pages\". And earlier \"OIML Basic Type Evaluation Report\". So role: \"type_evaluation_report\". pages: 42.\n\nrevision_history: not present — omit.\n\nfootnotes: none present — omit.\n\nMarking info: could go in description? There's a \"Marking and inscriptions\" section. The schema has no dedicated place. I could add to certified_type.description a summary: \"Marked with unit of measurement (m³), Q3 value and Q3/Q1 ratio, OIML certificate number, manufacturer's name, year of manufacture, serial number, flow direction arrow, MAP, letter H, temperature class T30, pressure loss class, installation sensitivity class.\" Good to include briefly.\n\nDate issued: \"2016-05-26\".\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, amendment: null (omit), scheme: \"B\" (basic certificate). Hmm — recommendation.scheme? The schema lists scheme under recommendation too. The R49-1/2/3 parts... scheme here likely \"basic\". I'll put \"B\".\n\naccuracy_classes: [2] or [\"2\"]? Probably strings or numbers; I'll use [2]... Schema example: accuracy_classes[]. I'll use [\"2\"]? Numbers seem cleaner: [2]. Hmm, accuracy class 2 is numeric-ish. I'll use [2].\n\nNow let me double-check secondary meter for Table 2 columns: \"Secondary meter: TU1M20 DN20 | Aquadis+ P1 DN20 (colspan 2) | TU1M20 DN20 | Aquadis+ P1 DN20 (colspan 2)\". So col1 TU1M20, col2-3 Aquadis+P1, col4 TU1M20, col5-6 Aquadis+P1. Primary: Woltex WE65 (col1-3), Woltex WE100 (col4-6) — interesting: WE100 for DN80. OK.\n\nTable 3: Primary Woltex WE100 all; Secondary: col1 TU1M20 DN20; cols2-5 TU1M25 DN25 (colspan 4); col6 \"Aquadis+P111 DN25\".\n\nTable 4: Primary WE150 all; Secondary: cols1-4 TU1M25 DN25 (colspan 4); col5 \"TU1 40F DN40\"; cols6-7 \"P290+ DN40\" (colspan 2).\n\nQ4 for DN65: 78.75 (63×1.25). Good.\n\nLengths Table 2: secondary meter: 190 for all six; combination: 300 (DN65 cols1-3), 350 (DN80 cols4-6). From the continuation table on page 5 top: \"Length [mm] - secondary meter: 190 | 190 | 190 | 190; Length combination: 300 | 300 | 350 | 350\" — that's 4 cells covering 6 columns via colspans: cell1=190 (col1), cell2=190 (colspan2, cols2-3), cell3=190 (col4), cell4=190 (colspan2, cols5-6). Combination: 300 (col1), 300 (cols2-3), 350 (col4), 350 (cols5-6). Yes.\n\nResolution secondary Table 2: col1 \"0.02 or 0.05\" (TU1M20), cols2-3 \"0.02\", col4 \"0.02 or 0.05\", cols5-6 \"0.02\". Yes.\n\nIndicating range secondary Table 2: all 99999. Primary: \"999 999 or 9 999 999\".\n\nTable 3 lengths: secondary: col1 190, cols2-5 260, col6 260. Combination: 350 all. Res secondary: col1 \"0.02 or 0.05\", cols2-6 \"0.02\". Indicating range secondary: 99999 all.\n\nTable 4: secondary length: cols1-5 260, cols6-7 300. Combination: 500 all. Indicating range primary: \"9 999 999 or 99 999 999\". Secondary: cols1-5 \"99 999\", cols6-7 \"999 999\". Resolution primary: \"2 or 20 (where 20 dm³ can be used up to Q1 = 3.125 m³/h included)\". Secondary: cols1-5 \"0.02\", cols6-7 \"0.50\".\n\nPressure loss class: Table1: 25; Table2: 40 (both DN65 and DN80); Table3: 25; Table4: 25.\n\nMaximum admissible pressure: 16 [MPa] everywhere. Temperature 30 °C. Note the marking mentions MAP10 or MAP16 — fine.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: Table1 U0S D0; Table2 U0S D0; Table3 U0S D0; Table4 U5S D0.\n\nNow build the JSON. Let me be careful with the models[].attributes — include primary_meter, secondary_meter. Maybe also dn in label.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider putting primary_meter/secondary_meter in models[].attributes vs model_level. The schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. attributes{} is free-form. I'll do:\n\n{\"model_id\": \"DN50-TU1M20-1600\", \"label\": \"ISOFLO DN50 combination meter, Woltex WE50 + TU1M20 DN20, Q3 40 m³/h, Q3/Q1 1600\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter\": 50, \"primary_meter\": \"Woltex (WE50)\", \"secondary_meter\": \"TU1M20 DN20\"}}\n\nHmm that duplicates nominal_diameter with model_level. I'll drop nominal_diameter from attributes (it's in model_level). Keep label concise: \"ISOFLO DN50, WE50 + TU1M20 DN20, Q3/Q1 = 1600\".\n\nLet me reconsider whether to even include the q1..q4 in BOTH matrix_tables and model_level. Yes, per rules. OK.\n\nActually, to manage size, maybe I can make matrix table rows leaner: rows keyed by variant id with only per-column data: q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, secondary_meter, length_secondary, length_combination, resolution_secondary, indicating_range_secondary (table4). Shared per-table stuff (primary meter per column though — varies within table 2 between WE65/WE100... it's per-column-group) — include primary_meter per row too. DN per row. qx1/qx2 same within table — include in description. Pressure loss class, connection, flow profile same within each table (table 2 has DN65/DN80 split: connection differs flange DN65 vs DN80 — that correlates with DN; I can put \"flange DN65/DN80\" or include per row). To keep faithful, include per-row: variant, nominal_diameter, primary_meter, secondary_meter, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, qx1, qx2, length_secondary, length_combination, resolution_secondary. And put connection/flow profile/pressure loss/indicating range primary/resolution primary in table description (they're uniform per table except noted). Table 4: indicating range secondary and resolution secondary vary → per row or description; res secondary per row (0.02 vs 0.50); ind range secondary varies cols6-7 → include per row for table 4.\n\nAlright. Description strings for tables:\n- Table 1: \"All variants: accuracy class 2, temperature class T30, pressure loss class 25, MAP 16 MPa, MAT 30 °C, orientation H, indicating range primary 999999 or 9999999 m³, indicating range secondary 99999 m³, resolution primary 0.2 or 2 dm³, connection flange DN50, flow profile sensitivity class U0S D0, EUT category 'Positive displacement meters and turbine water meters', case A.\"\n- Table 2: similar with pressure loss 40, DN65 rows: flange DN65, combination length 300; DN80 rows: flange DN80, length 350. qx1 0.75, qx2 3.\n- Table 3: pressure loss 25, flange DN100, U0S D0, qx1 1.8, qx2 4.2, combination length 350, primary range/res as DN50-100.\n- Table 4: pressure loss 25, flange DN150, U5S D0, qx1 4.5, qx2 12, combination length 500, primary indicating range 9999999 or 99999999, primary resolution \"2 or 20 (20 dm³ up to Q1 = 3.125 m³/h included)\".\n\nHmm wait, but I also planned config_level for these. There's redundancy between table descriptions and config_level. That's fine — config_level is the structured place; descriptions just preserve context. Maybe skip repeating in description to save space; keep descriptions one-liner: \"Basic technical parameters of water meters type ISOFLO DN 50 (3 variants)\". OK.\n\nLet me now also decide: include `indicating_range_secondary` in config_level: conditions \"DN50–DN100\" → 99999; \"DN150, secondary TU1M25 DN25 or TU1 40F DN40\" → 99999; \"DN150, secondary P290+ DN40\" → 999999.\n\nconfig_level entry format per schema: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. With rule 5: values follow structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll add unit_symbol at entry level for numeric ones, null otherwise.\n\nEntries:\n1. {attribute: \"qx1\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [{condition: \"DN50, DN65, DN80\", value: 0.75}, {condition: \"DN100\", value: 1.8}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: 4.5}]}\n2. {attribute: \"qx2\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", values: [{condition: \"DN50, DN65, DN80\", value: 3}, {condition: \"DN100\", value: 4.2}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: 12}]}\n3. {attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN50\", value: 25}, {condition: \"DN65\", value: 40}, {condition: \"DN80\", value: 40}, {condition: \"DN100\", value: 25}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: 25}]}\n4. {attribute: \"connection_type\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN50\", value: \"flange DN50\"}, ...]}\n5. {attribute: \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", values: [{condition: \"DN50 to DN100\", value: \"U0S D0\"}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: \"U5S D0\"}]}\n6. {attribute: \"indicating_range_primary\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", unit_symbol: \"m³\", values: [{condition: \"DN50 to DN100\", value: \"999999 or 9999999\"}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: \"9999999 or 99999999\"}]}\n7. {attribute: \"indicating_range_secondary\", axis: \"nominal_diameter and secondary meter\", unit_symbol: \"m³\", values: [{condition: \"DN50 to DN100\", value: 99999}, {condition: \"DN150 with TU1M25 DN25 or TU1 40F DN40\", value: 99999}, {condition: \"DN150 with P290+ DN40\", value: 999999}]}\n8. {attribute: \"resolution_of_indicating_device_primary\", axis: \"nominal_diameter\", unit_symbol: \"dm³\", values: [{condition: \"DN50 to DN100\", value: \"0.2 or 2\"}, {condition: \"DN150\", value: \"2 or 20 (20 dm³ usable up to Q1 = 3.125 m³/h included)\"}]}\n9. {attribute: \"resolution_of_indicating_device_secondary\", axis: \"secondary meter\", unit_symbol: \"dm³\", values: [{condition: \"TU1M20 DN20\", value: \"0.02 or 0.05\"}, {condition: \"TU1M25 DN25, Aquadis+ P1 DN20, Aquadis+ P111 DN25, TU1 40F DN40\", value: \"0.02\"}, {condition: \"P290+ DN40\", value: \"0.50\"}]}\n\nNote: \"999 999 or 9 999 999\" — these are counter ranges (max registration). I'll write as strings \"999999 or 9999999\".\n\nmodel_level entries (with model_ids):\n1. nominal_diameter: unit_symbol null (or \"mm\"). DN